            <h2>What makes an item worth recommending?</h2>
            <p>I look for supplies that can serve more than one unit, are easy to organize, are safe for students to use as directed, and have a low-cost or paper-based alternative whenever possible.</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Begin with the learning goal:</strong> choose a material because students need to sort, build, explain, display, or practice—not simply because it looks good.</li>
              <li><strong>Prefer repeated use:</strong> durable, magnetic, movable, and erasable materials often offer more value than one-time decorations.</li>
              <li><strong>Keep an accessible alternative:</strong> index cards, sticky notes, folders, and reused paper can support many of the same classroom routines.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
